Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Euclidean Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Euclidean Infrageometry",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " studies natural geometric objects on arbitrary graph substrates using ",
 StyleBox["synthetic methods",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". It enumerates these objects, studies their mutual relations, applies \
operations to them, and performs iterative constructions. If space is \
represented by a large graph, which we may view as a web of paths, human \
geometry can be viewed as an idealized theory based on our large-scale \
perception of ",
 StyleBox["infinitesimality",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". This leads to the idealization of space as a \"continuous web\" \
supporting idealized objects such as the \"breadthless line.\" In contrast, \
Infrageometry works directly with the discrete substrate at an observer's \
scale and aggregates instead of subdivides. Consequently, objects at the \
least scale may not be unique, leading to ",
 StyleBox["multi-objects",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", so that constructions generate branching ",
 StyleBox["multiway systems",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". Although concrete representatives may differ across branches, the system \
is \"causally invariant\" in the sense that the dependency graph of \
construction steps is the same in every branch. We associate each \
multi-object with ",
 StyleBox["vertex and edge densities",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " that count the representatives containing each vertex or edge, obtaining a \
measure by dividing by the total number of representatives. We expect that, \
under a suitable notion of ",
 StyleBox["Gromov-Hausdorff convergence",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", these measures converge weakly to Dirac measures supported on classical \
\"thin\" geometric objects. We also expect that a suitable axiomatization of \
Euclidean infrageometry can be formulated and the notion of  Euclidean-like \
graphs can be defined so that subdivision and convergence converges to the \
continuous axiomatic Euclidean geometry."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "GuideFunctionsSection",
 CellID->1866139230,ExpressionUUID->"a0ea3657-4c83-4862-93c8-ecf80cebc774"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We use ",
 StyleBox["inert objects",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " such as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"InfraSegment", "[", 
   RowBox[{"p", ",", " ", "q"}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "2790d401-23c8-4923-a062-4895edeb6931"],
 ", independent of a graph substrate. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"InfraMeasurement", "[", 
   RowBox[{"g", ",", " ", "obj", ",", " ", "property"}], "]"}]], 
  "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "9c265c90-5be7-4f98-8565-87e635bde762"],
 " then computes a property of an object on a concrete graph. Each object \
also has a named sampler; for example, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"RandomInfraSegment", "[", 
   RowBox[{"g", ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"InfraSegment", "[", 
     RowBox[{"p", ",", " ", "q"}], "]"}], ",", " ", "k"}], "]"}]], 
  "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "3523f0e4-1c52-4f19-b414-d0f1f7c148a3"],
 " gives up to k shortest paths from p to q as vertex lists."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"19b420dd-b99a-48b5-9c0c-72a49b5cb924"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Segments, rays, lines and arcs have compact representations as directed \
acyclic graphs such that the ",
 StyleBox["chains correspond to individual representatives",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". This allows densities to be computed efficiently without enumerating \
every path."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"d604b422-e732-4f90-883f-d1ae5019838a"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Inspired by ",
 ButtonBox["GeometricScene",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://reference.wolfram.com/language/ref/GeometricScene.html"], 
    None}],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData["InfraGeometricScene"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "8674ead5-b4dc-461c-8132-07b1e97da990"],
 " lets us describe a construction through named objects and hypotheses. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"RandomInfraInstance", "[", 
   RowBox[{"scene", ",", " ", "graph"}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "bf271493-1f50-457d-89b5-add9520df35b"],
 " draws an admissible instance on a given substrate, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"InfraGeometricSceneViewer", "[", 
   RowBox[{"scene", ",", " ", "graph"}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "e7f0f396-aef3-47fd-be9f-00a5d0f3e36e"],
 " displays the construction step by step in both the causal and branchial \
directions."
}], "GuideText",ExpressionUUID->"edc26ab0-b401-42ca-9821-416e5d14c021"],

